  "result": "I found the bug and fixed it with a one-character change. All 8 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** in `shop/storage.py:17`, `save_order` is meant to remove any old row for the order being saved, keep everything else, then append the new row. The filter used `==` where it needed `!=`, so it did the reverse: it kept only the old copy of this order and dropped every other order. Each save therefore erased all orders except the one being saved, so `load()` only ever returned the last one.\n\n**Fix:**\n```python\nrows = [r for r in self.load() if r[\"id\"] != order.id]\n```\n\n**Tests:** I added `tests/test_storage.py` with two tests:\n- After saving two different orders, `load()` returns both.\n- Saving the same order again (after changing its state) updates its row instead of adding a second one, and the other order is still there.\n\nBoth new tests fail against the original code, so they would catch this bug if it came back.\n\nA hook blocked `git stash push`, so I ran that check on a temporary copy of the code instead. Nothing is committed.",
